This instance received multiple highly heterogeneous pairs without any submission. The examples changed different small structures and did not validate one shared relation applicable to the test. The key missed step was not simply requesting too many examples: after each result, the next action should have been chosen from a synthesized changed-mask/role ledger and a concrete test-relevant uncertainty. A further request is justified only when a named competing hypothesis and a specific possible observation can change the predicted output; otherwise stop spending demos and assess the evidence already available.

