{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md", "content": "# Cross-demo changed-mask audit

When demos appear visually unrelated (different dimensions, background colors, seed motifs, and output colors), first compare their exact input/output differences rather than assuming a fixed motif-copy rule. For every pair, record: (1) role-level colors (background, seed, painted color), (2) exact changed-cell coordinates and their bounding boxes, (3) cells left unchanged inside apparent motifs, (4) any connected components or repeated offsets involved, and (5) a concrete rule that predicts both changed and unchanged cells.

A plausible hypothesis is not validated by resemblance. Replay it across *all* demos and require exact agreement, including untouched noise and cells that retain the background. In this session, several demos had completely different grid sizes and seed shapes, and a guessed connected-zero flood-fill failed; that illustrates why a visual/connectedness guess must be checked against demo outputs before submission. After a failed answer, re-evaluate the rule itself and seek a demo that distinguishes explicit alternatives, rather than making another unsupported guess. Keep each demo request tied to a named uncertainty, since requests and wrong submissions both incur cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-rejection-recovery", "file": "references/repeated-fallback-loop.md", "content": "# Repeated fallback submissions after rejection

An incorrect submission rejects the complete grid and gives no localization. Repeating the same blank/background-only candidate, or making cosmetic edits to it, cannot add information and wastes attempt budget. Before retrying, identify a materially changed hypothesis and check it against every complete demo, including output shape, role colors, changed-cell mask, and preservation. If no revised rule is supported, request a demonstration only when a concrete observation could distinguish live alternatives and change the test prediction. With heterogeneous demos, compare functional roles and changed masks; do not let one visually salient example override the rest.
