When available demonstrations differ sharply in grid size, palette, and object structure, log each pair's role colors, exact changed-cell mask, untouched/identity regions, and any plausible structural trigger. Treat a rule as supported only if it reproduces complete outputs, not merely the salient changed motif.

Before requesting another pair, name the competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would distinguish them; ask only if that distinction can materially change the test prediction. Repeated requests without a targeted discriminator have cost but may not improve inference. In the same way, do not progress from one vivid example directly to a large speculative output: preserve the test input unless evidence supports a transformation. Keep this evidence-gathering discipline compatible with strict interfaces: each puzzle response still emits exactly one action JSON.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md"}
